# WhatsApp Bot

Connect the WhatsApp Cloud API so that people can query glucose and receive alerts in
WhatsApp. This page gives the steps for WhatsApp. For account linking, alert delivery and the
other parts that all platforms share, see [Chat Bots](/docs/bots).

WhatsApp messages go through Meta's Cloud API. You need a Meta developer account and a phone
number for the bot. The number cannot also be in use in the WhatsApp app on a phone.

## 1. Create a Meta app

1. Go to [Meta for Developers](https://developers.facebook.com/apps) and create an app.
2. For the app type, select **Business**.
3. In the app, add the **WhatsApp** product.
4. In **WhatsApp > API Setup**, add the phone number for the bot, or use the test number that
   Meta gives you.

## 2. Collect the credentials

| Field | Where to find it |
|-------|------------------|
| **App Secret** | **App settings > Basic > App Secret** |
| **Phone Number ID** | **WhatsApp > API Setup**. This is the ID of the number, not the phone number. |
| **Access Token** | **WhatsApp > API Setup**. See the warning below. |
| **Verify Token** | A value that you choose. Use a long random string. |

<Callout type="warning" title="The API Setup token expires">

  The access token on the <strong>API Setup</strong> page is temporary. It expires after
  24 hours, and then the bot stops. For production, create a System User in Meta Business
  Settings, give it access to the app, and generate a permanent token for it.

</Callout>

To make a verify token, you can use `openssl rand -hex 32`.

## 3. Configure Nocturne

Enter the four values with one of the two methods below. For the full workflow, see
[Chat Bots](/docs/bots).

### Option A: Admin UI

Go to **Platform > Administration > Integrations** and fill in the **WhatsApp** card:

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Access Token** | The access token from step 2 |
| **App Secret** | The app secret from step 2 |
| **Phone Number ID** | The phone number ID from step 2 |
| **Verify Token** | The verify token that you chose |

Select the **Enabled** switch, select **Save**, and restart the Nocturne web service.

### Option B: Environment variables

```bash
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=...
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=...
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=...
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=...
```

Restart Nocturne after you set them.

## 4. Configure the webhook

Save the credentials in Nocturne and restart it before you do this step. Meta checks the
webhook when you save it.

1. In the Meta app, go to **WhatsApp > Configuration**.
2. In the **Webhook** section, select **Edit**.
3. Set the **Callback URL** to the WhatsApp webhook of your instance:

   ```
   https://your-instance.com/api/v4/webhooks/whatsapp
   ```

4. Set the **Verify Token** to the same value that you entered in Nocturne.
5. Select **Verify and Save**.
6. Under **Webhook fields**, subscribe to **messages** and **user_id_update**.

<Callout type="warning" title="The verify tokens must be the same">

  If the verify token in Meta is different from the one in Nocturne, the webhook check fails.

</Callout>

Nocturne gets the **messages** field for each message that a person sends and for the status
of each message that the bot sends. It gets **user_id_update** when a person changes their
phone number. Then the person's link to Nocturne stays in use.

## 5. Link a WhatsApp account

WhatsApp has no slash command menu. People type the commands as messages.

1. From WhatsApp, send `/connect` to the bot's number.
2. Open the link that the bot sends, and sign in to Nocturne.
3. Send `/status`. The bot replies with the accounts that you linked.
4. Send `/bg` to get the latest glucose reading.

Send `/help` for the list of commands. If you send a message that is not a command, the bot
replies with the list.

## 6. Receive alerts in WhatsApp

1. Open an alert rule.
2. Under **Delivery channels**, add **WhatsApp DM**.

The alert goes to the WhatsApp account that you linked with `/connect`. The alert has an
**Acknowledge** button.

### The 24-hour window

WhatsApp lets the bot send a message to a person only for 24 hours after that person last
sent a message to the bot. After that time, WhatsApp delivers only a message template that
Meta approved.

<Callout type="warning" title="Alerts outside the window">

  Without an approved template, an alert to a person who did not send a message to the bot in
  the last 24 hours fails. Nocturne shows the failure on the delivery. Send any message to the
  bot to open the window again, or configure an alert template.

</Callout>

To deliver alerts outside the window, configure an alert template:

1. In [WhatsApp Manager](https://business.facebook.com/wa/manage/message-templates/), create a
   template in the **Utility** category.
2. Write a body that has three variables:
   - `{{1}}` is the person.
   - `{{2}}` is the name of the alert.
   - `{{3}}` is the glucose reading, with its unit and trend.
3. Add one **Quick reply** button, for example *Acknowledge*.
4. Send the template to Meta for approval.
5. After Meta approves it, go to **Platform > Administration > Integrations** in Nocturne.
6. In the **WhatsApp** card, enter the **Alert Template Name** and the **Alert Template
   Language**, for example `en_US`.
7. Select **Save**, and restart the Nocturne web service.

If you do not enter a language, Nocturne uses `en`. The language must be the same as the
language of the approved template.

Example body:

```
Nocturne alert for {{1}}: {{2}}. Glucose: {{3}}.
```

When the window is closed, Nocturne sends the alert as this template. The quick reply button
acknowledges the alert, as the button on a normal alert does. A notice that a rule cannot run
or is on hold is not sent as a template.

<Callout type="info" title="Meta charges for templates">

  Meta charges for each template message that the bot sends. Messages in the 24-hour window
  have no charge.

</Callout>
